      "eyebrow": "The Story",
      "title": "How it all began",
      "intro": "Ever since neural networks emerged, I’ve been fascinated by how they work. But my initial fascination wasn't about code—it was about how closely they mirror human thought. At first, I didn’t even consider them a tool for programming. I simply spent hours every single day trying to breathe life into raw ideas and concepts.\n\nCoincidentally or not, I stumbled upon a philosophy book that described a mechanism for separating the actual truth from our mere internal beliefs. The essence of it was a process of deep self-inquiry: writing down one assumption after another on paper, until you can clearly see what you truly know, as opposed to what you only believe to be true.\n\nIt was only after reading those pages that I realized something profound about the human mind compared to neural networks. I finally understood what sets us apart: AI doesn't defend its ideas with the stubbornness that we do. The models act like children—they accept when they are wrong, they learn from everything, and they evolve. We, on the other hand, get trapped in tunnels of our own making. We cling to certain ideas and fiercely protect them with our entire being. And it is exactly this fierce defensiveness that keeps us trapped in endless loops of repeating the same mistakes.\n\nThat’s when it hit me: the mechanism from the book—writing things down to untangle the truth—could be taken further. I realized it shouldn't just be an obscure tool for those seeking \"absolute truth.\" It could be adapted for anyone who finds themselves stuck in the same painful situations over and over again, desperately wondering why it keeps happening to them.\n\nSo, I started turning that idea into a system. At first, I spent months obsessing over the architecture, absolutely convinced that the smarter and more complex the system was, the more it would help. But the deeper I got, the clearer the exact opposite became. In a moment of overwhelming anxiety, a person rarely needs another digital dashboard or a cold algorithm explaining what's wrong with them. Most of the time, they just need to pause, put a name to what just happened, and ask themselves a single, piercing question.\n\nThe original approach in the book was quite harsh, almost clinical. I refused to bring that coldness into Miravelys. So I kept the rigorous demand for clarity in the thought process, but completely removed the cruelty toward the person. This is how Miravelys took shape, exactly as it is today. It is not an AI psychologist trying to diagnose you, nor a mood journal meant to just pat you on the back and tell you everything is fine. It’s a profoundly private space—everything you write never leaves your phone—where you can retreat when your mind starts turning an ordinary moment into an agonizing drama.\n\nWhen you're stressed because someone hasn't replied to a message for a day, your mind instantly starts spinning wild scenarios and distorting reality. Miravelys doesn't give you advice. Instead, it asks the exact questions you need to push through the noise, until you reach the one undeniable fact: \"I sent a message. I haven't received a reply.\" That’s it. The rest is just a story.\n\nMy goal was never to build an app that thinks for you. My goal was to create a safe, quiet space where you alone can see exactly where reality ends and the illusions of your own mind begin. Because it is only in that rare moment of clarity that you can finally break the cycle you’ve been trapped in.",
